Lightningpanda patch 0029: tracked wrappers, host objects whose cells need no destructor.

A DOM wrapper was a `JSCallbackObject` in the destructible callback-object space with a weak
handle beside it (JSWeakCreateOwned / JSWeakCreateKeptWhile): making one allocated the handle,
every collection visited the handle to ask its owner whether to keep the object, and ending
one cost a finalizer call per object from the sweep of its block, on whatever thread swept.
A page that builds and drops a thousand elements per task paid that a thousand times: the
sweep of destructible blocks and the weak-set visit were 12% of `dom.mutate.1k`.

`JSObjectMakeTrackedWrapper` (JSWeakPrivate.h) makes the object in a subspace of its own
(`trackedCallbackObjectSpace`) whose cells have no destructor, so their blocks are swept
without a call, and the heap keeps one entry per object (cell, host data, two host bytes) in
a flat array:

  - flags[0] is the host's: while nonzero the object is kept alive. A marking constraint
    ("Tw") reads the bytes of the entries; an eden collection reads those made since the last
    collection only, the others being old objects it cannot end.
  - flags[1] is the collector's: at the end of a collection the entries are walked once, and
    an object that was not marked gets the byte set and its data put on a list. The host
    reads the byte where it would have asked a weak handle, and takes the list in bulk when
    it likes (`JSContextGroupTakeDeadTrackedWrappers`); the address of the list's length
    (`JSContextGroupDeadTrackedWrapperCounter`) lets it ask "anything to take?" with a load.

The class must be immortal (patch 0028): nothing of the object then needs releasing but its
private property map, if it has one, which the walk takes and the mutator's thread destroys
(its keys are atom strings). `JSContextReleaseTrackedWrappers` clears flags[0] of a global
object's wrappers (a realm ending); `JSContextGroupTakeAllTrackedWrappers` ends every entry
for a host that is dropping its VM.

A class records whether its objects have anything to initialise (`objectsNeedInit`: an
initialize callback or convertToType in its chain), which `JSCallbackObject::init` walked the
chain to find out for every object made; a tracked wrapper reads the flag.

Nothing here is specific to a platform.

